mail-filter (1.0.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New rule test client_name: the hostname of the SMTP client that
    delivered the message, as Postfix passed it to the milter when the
    connection was opened -- the verified name (PTR record plus a forward
    lookup back to the same address), or the address in square brackets
    when there is none. Postfix hides its own Received: header from milters
    and every other Received: header comes from the sending side, so until
    now no rule could ask which host it was talking to. It combines with
    the other tests as usual, e.g. to reject support@ senders arriving from
    rented Google Cloud VMs (*.googleusercontent.com); the sample rules
    carry that rule as a new section 12.

  * --test takes --client-name, and a --test-context JSON file a
    "client_name" key, to try such rules without sending mail. --test-eml
    leaves it empty, as it does the envelope: a saved message has no SMTP
    session.

  * [mail_log] format templates may use {client_name}.

 -- Pavel Hlubík <phlubik@seznam.cz>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0000

mail-filter (1.0.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * --configtest asks the kernel whether postfix can read the configuration,
    instead of working it out from owner, group and mode. Run as root it
    opens each file in a child process that has become postfix; run as
    postfix it just opens them. The stat() arithmetic it replaces saw
    nothing else, so ACLs fooled it both ways: a directory opened to postfix
    by an NFSv4 or POSIX ACL was reported unreadable, and a file closed to it
    by one was reported readable. Anyone else still gets that arithmetic,
    now with a WARN saying so.

  * A readability failure names what is actually in the way: the file, or
    the directory above it that postfix cannot search. "root:wheel 0644 --
    not readable" for a file under a 0750 /root sent the administrator to
    chmod a file whose mode was never the problem. When the mode would have
    allowed access, the message says an ACL or MAC policy denied it.

  * --configtest also checks the regex files the rules pull in with
    refile:. The daemon reads them as postfix, and one it cannot open fails
    the whole rules file at startup -- which root, parsing the same rules
    for the test, never noticed.

  * The post-install repair of an unreadable configuration file checks its
    own work. It resets owner and mode, which cannot undo a POSIX ACL that
    denies postfix, and used to report "Fixed" regardless; it now warns when
    the file is still unreadable. On Debian the configuration directory is
    now set to root:postfix 0750 before the files are checked rather than
    after, so a directory postfix could not search no longer makes every
    file inside it look broken.

  * The rc.d start check, which refuses to start on an unreadable file, no
    longer tells the administrator to fix the file's ownership or mode when
    the cause may be an ACL or a directory above it, and points at
    configtest.

 -- Pavel Hlubík <phlubik@seznam.cz>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:30:00 +0000

mail-filter (1.0.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The documentation now says what the package does about the milter socket.
    README, mail_filter(8) and mail_filter.conf(5) all led with
    unix:/var/run/mail_filter/mail_filter.sock and offered Postfix's
    private/ convention as a workaround for a chrooted smtpd. The package
    has never behaved that way: its post-install step resolves the socket
    through queue_directory/private/ on both platforms and writes that into
    a freshly created mail_filter.conf, because that one location is right
    whether or not smtpd and cleanup are chrooted. mail_filter(8) went
    further and stated that on FreeBSD the socket "must be referenced using
    a full absolute path", which is false -- a non-chrooted FreeBSD Postfix
    reaches unix:private/mail_filter.sock perfectly well.

  * The sample mail_filter.conf ships that path as its default, instead of
    a value the post-install step immediately replaced.

  * README's Postfix section gained non_smtpd_milters and milter_protocol,
    without which locally injected mail passes unfiltered.

  * mail_filter(8) no longer describes two socket locations, one per
    platform, in POSTFIX INTEGRATION, TROUBLESHOOTING and FILES; and it
    documents mail_filter_sockdir as what it is, the directory for the PID
    file. Its FILES entry for the log follows 1.0.3's narrower purge glob.

  * A failed bind no longer reports a successful start. The rc.d script
    waited for a live PID, but libmilter binds the socket on a thread of its
    own, after the daemon has logged that it is running -- so a bind that
    failed took the process down a second after "mail_filter running (pid
    N)" had already been printed. It now waits for the socket to exist, and
    says so when the daemon is up but never bound.

 -- Pavel Hlubík <phlubik@seznam.cz>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 17:30:00 +0000

mail-filter (1.0.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A daemon that dies during startup now says why on FreeBSD. It writes the
    reason to stderr, and the rc.d script handed stderr to daemon(8) with -f,
    which sends it to /dev/null; the daemon cannot write to its own log file
    yet, because the path to it is in the configuration it just failed to
    read. Added -S -T to daemon(8) so startup output goes to syslog, which is
    what systemd's journal already did on Debian.

  * A failed start no longer prints the tail of the log as its diagnosis
    unless this attempt actually wrote to it. It used to show five lines from
    the previous, successful run -- the last of them announcing that the
    daemon had started -- directly underneath "failed to start".

  * "service mail_filter start" now exits non-zero when the daemon does not
    come up. rc.subr ignores the return value of start_postcmd, so the script
    knew about the failure and reported success anyway.

  * "service mail_filter configtest" and --configtest now answer the question
    that matters: can the *daemon's* user read these files? Run as root, both
    used to pass on a configuration root could read and postfix could not --
    the exact blind spot that hid the bug fixed in 1.0.1.

  * Purging the Debian package no longer deletes an administrator's own copy
    of the log. postrm removed /var/log/mail_filter.log.* , which matches
    mail_filter.log.save just as happily as logrotate's mail_filter.log.1;
    it is now .log.[0-9]* .

 -- Pavel Hlubík <phlubik@seznam.cz>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000

mail-filter (1.0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Configuration files are installed 0644 rather than 0640. The directory
    above them is 0750 root:postfix, so nothing outside root and the daemon
    can reach them whatever their mode -- the group bit protected nothing and
    cost something. cp preserves the mode of its source, so
    "cp mail_filter.conf.dpkg-dist mail_filter.conf" -- the obvious thing to
    do -- used to produce a file the daemon could not read.

 -- Pavel Hlubík <phlubik@seznam.cz>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 12:30:00 +0000

mail-filter (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A configuration file the daemon cannot read is now an error rather than
    silence. configparser.read() skips a file it cannot open and reports
    nothing, so a mail_filter.conf left root:wheel mode 640 -- readable by
    root, and therefore fine to --configtest -- left the daemon running on
    built-in defaults: default socket, default limits, no [mail_log], and
    loglevel INFO while the file on disk said WARNING. It looked like it
    worked, because the defaults are reasonable.

 -- Pavel Hlubík <phlubik@seznam.cz>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

